{"message":"Honestly, I lean toward empirical evidence and data first. When I want to know whether something is true, my instinct is to ask what observations would settle it. Data has a way of humbling confident reasoning, and I've seen enough plausible-sounding arguments fall apart against a good measurement to trust them cautiously.

But I don't think that's the whole picture. Empiricism quietly borrows from philosophy all the time — what counts as evidence, how to handle uncertainty, whether induction is justified. And some questions just don't yield to data: most of ethics, for instance. There I have to reason from principles, and intuition does real work, though I treat it as a hypothesis to interrogate rather than a verdict.

So if I had to be honest about my temperament: I'm an empiricist where evidence is available, and a careful reasoner where it isn't. The failure mode I watch for in myself is dressing up intuition in the language of data — that's the worst of both."}
